  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nTODAY...We start this morning off with a corridor of storms  \nstretching from eastern CO all the way up through Lake Superior\nand into Canada. These storms have been heavy rainfall \nproducers with spots like KSTC recording periods of 2.5-3\"/hr \nrates. The airport saw 0.5\" fall in about 10 minutes. \nFortunately, from a flooding perspective, these storms have been\nfairly progressive tonight, with the main line already \napproaching the MN/WI border as of 3:15AM. The LLJ does not \nreally start to wane until after sunset, so continued \ndevelopment behind the main line will remain possible given that\nthere is still plenty of moisture to work with throughout \natmosphere. At the very least, the shield of precip near the \nSD/IA/MN corner will slowly spread northeast and bring on-and-\noff showers behind the main line. The bulk of the rain will be \ndone by late morning, though spotty showers may persist until \nthe front finally swings through today. \n\nThere is a growing signal for storms to redevelop along the\nfront this afternoon/evening as it moves from west to east. \nSufficient daytime heating and anomalously high PWAT values \nwill be in place ahead of the fropa, though the limiting factor \nwill be the absence of appreciable forcing and low-level \nconvergence. The best environment remains across Iowa, where the\nLLJ will enhance low- level shear. That said, you do see some \nsolutions like the 06z HRRR producing storms as far north as \nthe Twin Cities metro this evening. Weak mid-level lapse rates \nshould limit any severe threat if any isolated storms develop, \nhighlighted by the removal of SPC's Day 1 Marginal Risk for much\nof east-central MN and western WI.\n\nWEDNESDAY THROUGH THURSDAY...It'll be a quieter two-day period\nas higher pressure spreads in from the west. Winds will become\nbreezy on Wednesday as CAA supports gusts of around 25 to 35 \nmph to mix down to the surface. Highs will generally be in the \nupper 70s, but lows should drop into the 50s (or even 40s for \nsome spots north of I-94 Thursday morning).\n\nTHIS WEEKEND...Trends continue to favor a bit more activity both \nFriday and Sunday this weekend as an area of lower heights\nbuilds in near the international border. A series of frontal \npassages could support multiple chances for rain, including \nFriday evening and much of Sunday. There is uncertainty with \nthese setups being a few days out, but the SPC is at least \nhighlighting a window for severe potential with the Friday \nfropa. Then with Sunday, ensemble guidance is hinting at some \nlegit rainfall potential as a boundary stalls out across the \nUpper MS Valley. Expect future discussions to track these risks,\nthough for now there are many details still unknown and it is \ncertainly not worth changing any weekend plans quite yet.",
